.TH LYFY.CONF 5 "2026-10-29" "lyfy" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
lyfy.conf \- configuration and launch settings for lyfy
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lyfy
reads TOML settings from
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/lyfy/config.toml
or, by default,
.IR ~/.config/lyfy/config.toml .
If no readable user file exists, it searches the directories in
.BR XDG_CONFIG_DIRS ,
defaulting to
.IR /etc/xdg .
The first readable file wins as a whole; files from multiple directories
are not merged.
.PP
.B \-\-config_file
or
.B LYFY_CONFIG_FILE
selects one required file directly.
A missing or unreadable explicitly selected file is an error.
Unknown keys and malformed values are errors.
.PP
The manual is named lyfy.conf(5); the configuration file is named config.toml.
.SH PRECEDENCE
Each setting below accepts a
.B \-\-snake_case
global option and a
.B LYFY_UPPER_CASE
environment variable.
For example,
.B require_uki
corresponds to
.B \-\-require_uki
and
.BR LYFY_REQUIRE_UKI .
.PP
Precedence is command-line option, environment, TOML, then default.
Boolean overrides take an explicit true or false value.
List overrides are comma-separated and replace the whole lower-priority list.
.B \-\-write
is execution permission for apply/fmt, not a setting.
It cannot be supplied through TOML or ENV.
.B \-\-elevate
is a lint launch policy, not a setting, and has no ENV equivalent.
Its default auto mode requests sudo for interactive scans of the running
system; never disables the request and required forces it.
It preserves resolved user settings when invoking sudo for a protected scan.
.SH SETTINGS
.TP
.B system
System profile.
Only
.B arch
is supported; it is the default.
.TP
.B root
Absolute physical root of the managed filesystem tree.
The default is
.IR / .
Runtime checks of the running kernel, EFI and systemd
are skipped explicitly for an alternative root.
.TP
.B home_dir
Absolute logical home path inside root.
The default comes from HOME.
Saved home paths map to this destination path and its owner's uid/gid.
.TP
.B data_dir
Absolute local path to the state repository, independent of root.
The default is
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/lyfy
or
.IR ~/.local/share/lyfy .
.TP
.B state_dir
Absolute local path for receipts, locks and recovery journals.
The default is
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/lyfy
or
.IR ~/.local/state/lyfy .
It must differ from data_dir.
.TP
.B storage_format
.B git
(default) stores ordinary host payload files and JSON metadata in a Git
worktree.
.B directory
uses immutable JSON snapshots and SHA-256 content objects.
Export/import provides explicit migration into a new destination.
.TP
.B mounts, backends, selected_mounts
Named VFS mount policies and Git/directory/S3 destinations.
Mounts have name, path, backend, history (retained/current_only), retain,
apply, packages, exclude, max_file_bytes, replicas and replicas_required fields.
Backends have name, kind, optional local path, optional S3 connection and public
age recipients. S3 connection fields are bucket, prefix, endpoint, region and
optional profile. Nested paths use longest component-prefix ownership.
Mount/backend CLI and ENV overrides accept a complete JSON array.
selected_mounts accepts comma-separated names including default.
Data mounts participate in ordinary apply only when apply=true; explicit
selection permits their restoration. Lists replace lower-priority lists.
.TP
.B export_file, import_file
Complete portable archive destination/source. Select one scope.
Save/import publishes it; apply/import uses a private temporary source.
Existing exports are never replaced. Encrypted sources produce age archives.
.TP
.B identity_files, protected_paths
Absolute connection identity paths and additional protected physical paths.
These input-owned lists have LYFY_IDENTITY_FILES/LYFY_PROTECTED_PATHS overrides.
Active identities and AWS shared credential files are always excluded.
Directory/S3 recipients select native age encryption; Git remains readable.
Credentials are read from AWS ENV or a shared profile, never from snapshots.
.TP
.B host
Host identifier, defaulting to the trimmed contents of
.IR /etc/hostname .
Identifiers use ASCII letters, digits, hyphen, underscore or dot, cannot
start with a dot and contain at most 128 bytes.
.TP
.B base_host
Optional host whose current saved state becomes the pinned base of a new
save.
It must differ from host.
An empty override clears this setting.
.TP
.B snapshot
Full committed revision or snapshot ID to apply.
The default is
.BR latest .
Save always selects latest and creates or reuses state according to the
storage backend.
Uncommitted Git payload changes are not deployed.
.TP
.B include
Managed file/directory prefixes.
Defaults are the current XDG_CONFIG_HOME, ~/.bashrc, ~/.bash_profile,
~/.zshrc, ~/.profile, ~/.gitconfig, /etc and /usr/local/etc.
Missing optional paths are skipped.
.TP
.B exclude
Prefixes omitted from capture and reconciliation.
Defaults include ~/Projects, ~/.cache, ~/.local/state, ~/.ssh, ~/.gnupg, ~/.aws,
/etc/shadow, /etc/shadow-, /etc/gshadow, /etc/gshadow-, /etc/ssh,
/etc/ssl/private, /etc/credstore, /etc/credstore.encrypted,
/etc/pacman.d/gnupg and /etc/machine-id.
Associated pacnew/pacsave files inherit the active file's exclusion.
.TP
.B ownership_roots
Trees scanned for files not recorded in pacman's local ownership inventory.
Defaults are /usr/bin, /usr/lib and /usr/share.
.TP
.B require_uki
Require Type #2 UKI boot entries and no Type #1 entries.
The default is true.
This is a lyfy policy rather than an Arch Linux requirement.
.TP
.B check_packages
Capture, compare and inspect package state.
The default is true.
Classification uses configured sync repositories in the target pacman.conf.
It is unknown when no target pacman.conf is available.
.TP
.B install_packages
Allow supported package restoration during apply with
.BR \-\-write .
The default is false.
Execution requires existing root privileges and text display.
Apply does not invoke sudo.
.TP
.B overwrite
Permit replacements that conflict with live content or the last applied
receipt.
The default is false.
Review the apply preview before enabling it.
.TP
.B prune
Permit deletion of explicitly tracked paths absent from desired state.
The default is false.
Untracked files are retained and directory deletion is nonrecursive.
.TP
.B max_file_bytes
Maximum regular-file bytes during capture and planning.
The default is 0, unlimited locally. Content streams with bounded buffers.
S3 single payloads have a separate 5 GiB limit including encryption overhead.
.TP
.B capture_errors
strict (default) blocks incomplete snapshot publication.
Explicit skip records diagnostics and returns exit 2. Incomplete snapshots
cannot be applied. Previously saved unreadable paths are not deletions.
.TP
.B disabled_rules
List of validated rule IDs excluded from inspection; default empty.
For example, ["arch.orphans"]. Checks with all rules disabled do not run.
This applies to lint and the inspection used by fmt.
.TP
.B fail_on
Minimum severity that makes a complete inspection fail:
.BR info , warning " (default), or " error .
Lower-severity findings remain visible. Incomplete coverage always returns 2.
.TP
.B format
CLI result format: text (default), json, toml, yaml, markdown or sarif.
Markdown and SARIF are lint-only. Interactive package execution requires text.
This preference is not passed to the library.
.TP
.B output_file
Optional physical destination for a lint report, independent of root.
The parent must exist. Files are private (0600), published atomically and never
replace an existing node. The default is stdout; - explicitly selects stdout.
Reports from automatically elevated scans are saved by the original caller.
This preference is not passed to the library.
.TP
.B progress
Lint feedback: auto (default), plain or never.
Auto uses an updating terminal display when stderr is a terminal and TERM is
set and not dumb. Otherwise it uses plain stage messages and package counts.
NO_COLOR disables colors. Never hides scan feedback.
Progress always uses stderr and never enters report files.
This preference is not passed to the library.
.SH PATH RULES
Scope-list paths must be absolute or start with
.BR ~/ .
They are prefixes, not globs.
The tilde maps to home_dir; absolute target paths are resolved inside root.
data_dir and state_dir are physical local paths and are not prefixed by root.
There is no shell or general environment interpolation.
.PP
Virtual/runtime/cache trees /proc, /sys, /dev, /run, /tmp, /var/tmp,
/var/cache and /var/lib/pacman are always excluded from file state.
The data and state repositories themselves are also excluded.
The root and home directory nodes are not restored as files, but their
children may be managed through / or ~ scopes.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
system = "arch"
host = "laptop"
storage_format = "git"
base_host = "common"
require_uki = true
check_packages = true
install_packages = false
overwrite = false
prune = false
max_file_bytes = 0
format = "text"
progress = "auto"
fail_on = "warning"
disabled_rules = []
.fi
.PP
Omit include/exclude to retain the defaults and XDG_CONFIG_HOME handling.
.SH XDG DIRECTORIES
Relative XDG base-directory values are ignored.
Unset or empty variables use their standard HOME defaults.
New application directories use mode 0700 and files use mode 0600.
Existing XDG parent directories are not chmodded.
An existing application repository/state directory must already be private
and owned by the invoking user for mutating operations.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lyfy (1),
.BR git (1),
.BR pacman (8),
.BR bootctl (1)
